## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Preference memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` plus guidance-specific extensions.  Voice samples (quoted verbatim from the originator) are strongly recommended in the body — they make the preference internalize-able for future collaborators.

{{slot-table}}

## Canonical body structure

Preferences are less templated than patterns; they trend toward prose.  The established shape:

```markdown
## The filter

<One-paragraph summary of the preference.>

## Patterns / instincts

<The enumerated convictions (when the preference is a synthesis,
like a cross-library aesthetic with N enumerated points).>

## Voice samples

<Verbatim quotes that capture the register.  These are
load-bearing — paraphrasing drifts.>

## What this means for collaboration

<Concrete actions a future session can take to honor the
preference.>

## See also

<Related preferences and patterns that instantiate the preference.>
```

## Pitfalls

- **Moralizing without evidence** — a preference with no citation is just an opinion; ground preferences in the principal's own code / commits / conversations
- **Overlap with patterns** — if the preference is really a convention ("kernel-surface separation"), it's a pattern.  Preferences are tastes; patterns are rules
- **Stale voice samples** — voice evolves.  Re-audit quoted material on major reviews

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`pattern`** — the convention counterpart; preferences are softer (aesthetic) than patterns (structural)
- **`feedback`** — when a preference rises to a procedural rule
- **`protocol`** — when a preference becomes meta-level about the memory system itself
- **`library`** — library studies frequently surface preferences through aesthetic audits

